## 6. What Is Bias in ML?

Bias is a systematic distortion or unfairness in data, model behaviour, or outcomes.

### Bias Can Affect

```text
who is represented in data
which labels are treated as correct
which features are used
how errors are distributed
which groups are harmed
how model outputs are interpreted
```

### Example

A speech recognition model trained mostly on one accent may perform worse for users with other accents.

### Strong Exam Phrase

Bias in machine learning can occur when training data, labels, features, or model decisions systematically produce inaccurate or unfair outcomes for certain groups.

---

## 7. Data Bias

Data bias happens when the training data is not fair, complete, or representative.

### Causes

```text
some groups are missing or underrepresented
data comes from only one location or group
old data no longer represents current users
data collection method excludes some people
data contains historical unfairness
labels are subjective or inconsistent
```

### Example

A face recognition model trained mostly on one demographic group may perform worse for other groups.

### Effect

The model may work well for some people but poorly for others.

---

## 8. Sampling Bias

Sampling bias happens when the collected data does not represent the population where the model will be used.

### Example

A school builds a student risk model using data from only high-performing schools.

If used in all schools, the model may not work well for schools with different resources or student needs.

### Why It Matters

The model may learn patterns that only apply to the sample, not the wider population.

### Mitigation

```text
collect data from diverse and relevant groups
check who is missing from the dataset
test performance across groups
update data when real users change
```

---

## 9. Historical Bias

Historical bias happens when past data reflects unfair real-world decisions.

### Example

A hiring model trained on past hiring decisions may learn patterns from past discrimination.

Even if the algorithm is mathematically correct, it may repeat unfair history.

### Key Idea

Historical data is not automatically fair data.

### Mitigation

```text
review data sources
check historical decisions for unfairness
avoid blindly using past outcomes as ideal labels
use fairness testing
include human and ethical review
```

---

## 10. Label Bias

Label bias happens when the labels used for training are unfair, subjective, or incorrect.

### Example

A dataset labels student behaviour as "problematic" based on inconsistent teacher reports.

If some groups are more likely to be reported for the same behaviour, the labels may be biased.

### Effect

The model may learn to reproduce biased labels.

### Mitigation

```text
define labels clearly
use reliable labelling process
review labels for consistency
involve domain experts
check label distribution across groups
```

---

## 11. Feature Bias and Proxy Variables

A proxy variable is a feature that indirectly represents another attribute.

### Example

A model does not use income directly, but uses:

```text
postcode
school attended
shopping location
device type
```

These may indirectly reveal income or social background.

### Risk

Even if sensitive attributes are removed, proxy variables may still cause unfair outcomes.

### Exam Phrase

Removing a sensitive attribute does not always remove bias because other features may act as proxies for that attribute.

---

## 12. Algorithmic Bias

Algorithmic bias can occur when model design, optimization goals, or thresholds produce unfair outcomes.

### Examples

```text
model optimizes overall accuracy but ignores minority group performance
threshold creates many false positives for one group
model is tuned on validation data that is not representative
recommendation algorithm reinforces narrow content exposure
```

### Key Idea

Bias can come from both data and system design.

### Mitigation

```text
use suitable evaluation metrics
evaluate across groups
review thresholds
monitor deployed model outcomes
include ethical review
```

---

## 13. Bias in Model Evaluation

A model may have high overall performance but poor performance for one group.

### Example

```text
overall accuracy = 92%
group A accuracy = 96%
group B accuracy = 72%
```

### Problem

Overall accuracy hides unequal performance.

### Better Practice

Evaluate:

```text
performance across relevant groups
false positive rates across groups
false negative rates across groups
precision and recall across groups
real-world impact of errors
```

---

## 14. Fairness

Fairness means avoiding unjust or harmful treatment of people or groups.

### Fairness Questions

```text
Does the model work equally well for different groups?
Are some groups more likely to receive negative predictions?
Are errors more harmful for some users?
Are sensitive or proxy features being used unfairly?
Can affected users challenge the decision?
```

### Important

Fairness is not just a technical metric.  
It also depends on social context and consequences.

---

## 15. Ethics in Machine Learning

Ethics is about responsible and acceptable use of machine learning.

### Ethical Questions

```text
Should this model be built?
Should this model be used for this decision?
Who may be harmed?
Who benefits?
Can the decision be explained?
Who is responsible if the model is wrong?
Can users appeal or correct errors?
Is data collected fairly?
```

### Key Idea

Just because a model can be built does not mean it should be used.

---

## 16. Accountability

Accountability means someone is responsible for the system and its outcomes.

### Why It Matters

If a model wrongly rejects a loan, flags a student, or affects medical support, users need to know:

```text
who made the system
who approved its use
who monitors it
who corrects errors
who handles complaints
who is responsible for harm
```

### Good Practice

```text
clear ownership
audit logs
documentation
appeal process
human review
regular monitoring
```

---

## 17. Transparency

Transparency means being open about how data and models are used.

### Examples

Users may need to know:

```text
that an ML system is being used
what data is collected
why data is collected
what the model is used for
who can access the data
how long data is kept
how to challenge a decision
```

### Limitation

Full technical details may be hard to explain, but systems should still provide meaningful information.

---

## 18. Explainability

Explainability is the ability to understand why a model produced an output.

### Why It Matters

Explainability helps:

```text
users understand decisions
developers debug errors
organizations check fairness
teachers/doctors/staff review outputs
regulators/auditors inspect systems
```

### Example

A student risk model should not only say:

```text
high risk
```

It should ideally give useful reasons, such as:

```text
low attendance and missing assignments contributed to prediction
```

### Limitation

Some models are more difficult to explain than others.

---

## 19. Human Oversight

Human oversight means humans review, monitor, or approve model outputs.

### Needed Especially For

```text
medical decisions
student support decisions
loan approval
job screening
legal or policing decisions
insurance pricing
content moderation appeals
```

### Why?

Models can be:

```text
wrong
biased
outdated
hard to explain
missing context
based on poor data
```

### Good Use

ML should often support human decision-making rather than fully replace it in high-stakes contexts.

---

## 20. Automation Bias

Automation bias happens when people trust automated outputs too much.

### Example

A teacher sees that an ML system labels a student as "high risk" and accepts it without reviewing context.

### Risk

Humans may ignore their own judgement or other evidence.

### Mitigation

```text
train users to question model outputs
show confidence and limitations
require human review
provide explanations
allow appeals
monitor decisions
```

---

## 21. Privacy in Machine Learning

Privacy means personal data should be collected, used, stored, shared, and deleted responsibly.

### ML Privacy Risks

```text
large datasets may contain personal data
sensitive data may be exposed
data may be used without consent
data may be kept too long
data may be reused for a different purpose
model outputs may reveal private information
anonymized data may be re-identified
```

### Key Idea

ML data often comes from databases, apps, sensors, websites, or user behaviour, so privacy must be considered from the start.

---

## 22. Personal Data and Sensitive Data

### Personal Data

Personal data can identify a person directly or indirectly.

Examples:

```text
name
email
student ID
phone number
address
IP address
location history
face image
voice recording
account ID
purchase history
```

### Sensitive Data

Sensitive data may cause greater harm if exposed or misused.

Examples:

```text
health data
financial data
grades
discipline records
biometric data
precise location
political or religious information
disability information
passport/identity data
```

### Key Point

Sensitive data needs stronger protection and clearer justification.

---

## 23. Data Minimization

Data minimization means collecting only the data needed for a clear purpose.

### Example

A movie recommendation system may need:

```text
watch history
ratings
genre preferences
```

It probably does not need:

```text
passport number
medical history
private messages
exact home address
```

### Why It Matters

Less data reduces:

```text
privacy risk
breach impact
misuse risk
storage burden
legal/ethical responsibility
```

---

## 24. Consent and Purpose Limitation

Consent means users give permission for data collection or use.

Purpose limitation means data should be used only for the stated purpose.

### Example

If a school collects attendance data for student support, it should not use it for unrelated marketing.

### Good Practice

```text
explain what data is collected
explain why it is collected
get consent where needed
do not reuse data for unrelated purposes without proper permission
allow users to understand or challenge use
```

---

## 25. Anonymization and Pseudonymization

### Anonymization

Anonymization removes identifying information so a person cannot reasonably be identified.

### Pseudonymization

Pseudonymization replaces identifying information with a code.

Example:

```text
StudentID 10231 → Participant A17
```

A mapping may still exist, so identity could be recovered by someone with access to the mapping.

### Difference

| Method | Identity Recovery |
|---|---|
| anonymization | should not be reasonably possible |
| pseudonymization | possible if mapping key exists |

### Important

Anonymization can reduce risk, but it must be done carefully because re-identification may still be possible in some datasets.

---

## 26. Access Control and Encryption

ML datasets should be protected.

### Access Control

Only authorized people should access data.

Examples:

```text
data scientists can access training data if needed
teachers can access only their students' relevant records
administrators manage permissions
external users cannot access raw data
```

### Encryption

Encryption protects data confidentiality.

Use cases:

```text
data in transit
stored datasets
backups
model files if sensitive
logs
```

### Key Idea

Privacy is not only about rules. It also needs technical protection.

---

## 27. Retention and Deletion

Retention means how long data is kept.

### Good Practice

```text
do not keep data longer than necessary
delete old data securely
anonymize data when possible
define retention schedule
review stored datasets regularly
```

### Example

A school ML project should not keep old student behaviour data forever without a clear reason.

### Risk

Keeping unnecessary data increases harm if a breach happens.

---

## 28. Security and Privacy Link

Security and privacy are connected but different.

| Concept | Main Focus |
|---|---|
| Security | protect data from unauthorized access, loss, or damage |
| Privacy | responsible use of personal data |

### Example

A dataset can be secure but still unethical if:

```text
it collects unnecessary personal data
users did not consent
data is used for a different purpose
data is kept forever
```

### Key Idea

Good ML systems need both security and privacy.

---

## 29. High-stakes Decisions

High-stakes decisions can seriously affect people's lives.

Examples:

```text
medical diagnosis support
loan approval
job selection
student risk labelling
criminal justice risk tools
insurance pricing
immigration screening
```

### Extra Care Needed

```text
strong evaluation
bias testing
privacy protection
human oversight
appeal process
explainability
documentation
monitoring after deployment
```

### Key Idea

The higher the risk, the stronger the safeguards should be.

---

## 30. Reducing Bias and Ethical Risk

Possible methods:

```text
collect representative data
check data for missing groups
review labels for fairness
remove or carefully handle sensitive/proxy features
evaluate performance across groups
use suitable metrics, not only overall accuracy
monitor real-world outcomes
include human oversight
provide appeal mechanisms
document data sources and model limitations
```

### Important

Bias cannot always be fully removed, but it can be identified, reduced, monitored, and managed.

---

## 31. Reducing Privacy Risk

Possible methods:

```text
collect only necessary data
get consent where needed
use clear purpose limitation
anonymize or pseudonymize data
limit access to authorized users
encrypt data and backups
delete data when no longer needed
avoid unnecessary sensitive features
audit data access
train staff on privacy responsibilities
```

### Strong Exam Phrase

Privacy risk can be reduced through data minimization, consent, access control, encryption, anonymization or pseudonymization, and retention limits.

