## 42. Exam-style Questions

### Question 1 [4 marks]

Explain how bias can enter a machine learning system.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Bias can enter a machine learning system through unrepresentative training data, historical unfairness in past data, incorrect or subjective labels, poor feature selection, or proxy variables that indirectly represent sensitive attributes. The model may learn these biased patterns and produce unfair or inaccurate outcomes for some groups.

</details>

---

### Question 2 [5 marks]

A model has high overall accuracy but performs much worse for one group of users. Explain why this is an ethical concern.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

This is an ethical concern because the model may be unfair or less reliable for that group, even if the overall accuracy looks good. Users in that group may receive more incorrect decisions or be harmed more often. The system should be evaluated across relevant groups, not only by overall accuracy, and controls such as improved data, adjusted model design, and human oversight may be needed.

</details>

---

### Question 3 [6 marks]

A school wants to use ML to predict students at risk of failing. Discuss two privacy or ethical concerns and one control for each.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

One privacy concern is that the system may collect sensitive student data, such as grades, attendance, or behaviour records. A control is data minimization, collecting only data needed for support and protecting it with access control and encryption. One ethical concern is unfair labelling, where students may be treated negatively because of a prediction. A control is human oversight, so teachers review predictions and use them to provide support rather than punishment. The school should also check fairness across student groups and allow correction or appeal.

</details>

---

### Question 4 [6 marks]

Explain why removing sensitive attributes from a dataset may not fully remove bias.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Removing sensitive attributes may not fully remove bias because other features can act as proxy variables. For example, postcode, school attended, device type, or shopping location may indirectly reveal income, social background, or other sensitive attributes. The model may still learn patterns that disadvantage some groups. Therefore, the dataset and model outputs should be tested for fairness, not only checked for direct sensitive fields.

</details>

---

