## 45. One-page Revision Summary

| Point | Summary |
|---|---|
| Bias | Systematic unfairness or distortion |
| Data bias | Data is incomplete, unrepresentative, or unfair |
| Historical bias | Past unfairness appears in training data |
| Sampling bias | Sample does not represent real users |
| Label bias | Labels are incorrect, subjective, or unfair |
| Proxy variable | Indirectly represents a sensitive attribute |
| Fairness | Avoid unfair harm across people/groups |
| Ethics | Responsible and acceptable use of ML |
| Accountability | Humans/organizations responsible for outcomes |
| Transparency | Users understand data/model use |
| Explainability | Model outputs can be meaningfully explained |
| Human oversight | Human review of important predictions |
| Privacy | Responsible handling of personal data |
| Data minimization | Collect only necessary data |
| Consent | Permission/clear basis for data use |
| Anonymization | Remove identifying information |
| Pseudonymization | Replace identifiers with codes |
| Retention | Keep data only as long as needed |
| Key exam phrase | Biased or unrepresentative data can lead to unfair model outputs, so ML systems need fairness testing, privacy protection, transparency, accountability, and human oversight |

